UID:a5a29203f4c71d89@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260814T191151Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Continuing the Fight Against the Opi
 oid Epidemic
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nOver the five years leading up to 2019\, more than 1\,600 people in
  Maine died from drug overdoses. We were losing a person every day. About 
 8% of all babies in Maine were born substance-exposed—that's three each 
 day. The life-saving drug Naloxone was not generally available. There were
  few treatment beds\, and when someone was ready to get into recovery\, ev
 en fewer treatment options were available if they didn't have health insur
 ance.\nWhen I took office\, I knew that what Maine had been doing for so m
 any years just wasn't working. We needed a coordinated response that broke
  down the silos of government\, set aside the stigma\, and spoke out about
  every aspect of the opioid epidemic. I promised that we would put the ful
 l force of my Administration behind the families who lost loved ones\, the
  businesses who lost employees\, and all the communities that have been di
 minished by this public health crisis.\nSo my first executive order\, sign
 ed on my first day in office\, fulfilled the will of the people of Maine a
 nd expanded MaineCare to more than 90\,000 people. Today\, nearly 40\,000 
 people receive treatment for substance use disorder through MaineCare. Exp
 anding health insurance was a critical first step to confronting the opioi
 d crisis.\nI named Gordon Smith as my Director of Opioid Response\, and I 
 signed the second executive order with basic strategies and specific actio
 n steps. That order established a Prevention and Recovery Cabinet made up 
 of commissioners from 14 government agencies and the Attorney General's Of
 fice and the Judicial Branch.\nWe outlined key steps to preventing overdos
 es and deaths\, including making Naloxone widely available statewide\, and
  since early 2019\, we've distributed more than 870\,000 doses of Naloxone
 \, which has saved thousands of lives.\nWe then outlined steps to expand e
 ffective treatment and recovery measures\, including training 250 recovery
  coaches and funding recovery coaches in ten \nhttp://www.maine.gov/govern
 or/mills/news/radio_address/continuing-fight-against-opioid-epidemic-2026-
 08-14
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/continuing-figh
 t-against-opioid-epidemic-2026-08-14
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4b9ed0ce1e02be2b@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260807T191257Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Expanding Local Food Production and 
 Agriculture Education Across Maine
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nWell\, you know\, one of the lessons learned from the pandemic time
  was how dependent we are as a state and community on producers from other
  states and other countries for basic goods like food\, textiles\, and con
 struction materials. It occurred to me that our growing season is too incr
 edibly short for us to become as self-reliant as we should be.\nAnd after 
 the pandemic disrupted markets and caused food shortages nationwide\, we r
 ealized it was very important to have locally grown\, fresh food right her
 e in Maine and help our local farmers. So I used federal funding through m
 y Jobs and Recovery Plan to provide grants to farmers to buy equipment to 
 meet that higher demand for locally grown food\, and I began to look for o
 ther ways to extend our growing season here in Maine.\nWell\, one of the t
 hings you can do is to provide greenhouses to communities who are able to 
 support them and help people of all ages learn how to grow their own food.
  I called on the Legislature to provide one-time funds through the state b
 iennial budget to buy greenhouses for as many schools and communities as p
 ossible. That will strengthen our supply of local food and will teach kids
  and families to grow their own food and extend the growing season.\nThe L
 egislature funded that proposal\, and I'm proud to announce that we're now
  awarding grants to communities across Maine to expand their local food pr
 oduction and education programs through durable shared-use greenhouses. Th
 ese grants were awarded through a competitive process. The organizations a
 pplied for them\, and they include the Alfond Youth and Community Center i
 n Waterville\, where they're going to install a greenhouse to expand local
  food production and youth education.\nThe Healthy Island Project in Stoni
 ngton will use a greenhouse to increase access to fresh\, locally grown fo
 od. And the Portland Community Squash will install a greenhouse to send mo
 re fresh produce home to low-inco\nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/new
 s/radio_address/expanding-local-food-production-and-agriculture-education-
 across-maine-2026-08
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/expanding-local
 -food-production-and-agriculture-education-across-maine-2026-08
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c5d032d50abecc20@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260731T194936Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] New Laws Will Lower Costs\, Strength
 en Education\, and Create Opportunity
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nYou know\, even at a time when Washington\, D.C. seems unable to ge
 t much of anything done\, here in Maine\, we're proving once again that pe
 ople of goodwill can get together and solve problems. Working with people 
 of all parties and with members of the public\, we enacted laws this year 
 that will lower costs\, make it easier to rent or buy a home\, control chi
 ldcare costs\, strengthen education\, and above all\, create more opportun
 ity for Maine people.\nDozens of laws took effect this week\, including th
 e Supplemental Budget. Education\, of course\, as we know\, opens doors of
  opportunity\, so we focused on education. And that's why in 2022\, with t
 he support of the Legislature\, we made community college free for recent 
 high school grads. That free community college program has made a life-cha
 nging difference for thousands of young people in Maine\, and that's why I
  proposed\, and the Legislature approved\, making it permanent in this Sup
 plemental Budget.\nWe also raised teacher pay. We banned cell phones bell-
 to-bell in the public schools. In addition to all those measures\, the bud
 get gives $300 checks back to more than 500\,000 people in Maine to help o
 ffset\, in some small way\, the cost of gas\, goods\, and groceries that a
 re increasing every day because of circumstances we can't control—things
  like the war in Iran and national tariffs.\nThe budget builds on my Admin
 istration's historic investments in housing\, creating more than 400 new a
 ffordable homes for families all across Maine. Other laws extend our affor
 dable housing tax credit and limit rent increases on mobile homes and upda
 te permitting to make housing development easier. These laws also took eff
 ect this week. We can never do enough to create more housing\, but we're d
 oing everything we can.\nThe Supplemental Budget maintains the state's obl
 igation to pay 55 percent of basic local education costs to help hold down
  property taxes\, and it expands property tax relief\nhttp://www.maine.gov
 /governor/mills/news/radio_address/new-laws-will-lower-costs-strengthen-ed
 ucation-and-create-opportunity-2026-07-31
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/new-laws-will-l
 ower-costs-strengthen-education-and-create-opportunity-2026-07-31
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4baa724bc30ff71e@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260724T194435Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260724
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Maine’s 6th Annual Wild Blueberry 
 Weekend
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nRecently\, I was proud to\nproclaim\nAugust 1 and 2\, 2026\, as Mai
 ne's 6th annual Wild Blueberry Weekend. We do this to celebrate our wild b
 lueberries\, of course\, and in honor of the hard-working people who grow 
 and bring Maine blueberries to markets near and far.\nThe Wabanaki Nations
  in Maine were the first to harvest wild blueberries. In recent history\, 
 the Passamaquoddy Wild Blueberry Company\, headquartered in Columbia Falls
  and owned and operated by the Passamaquoddy Tribe\, has been managing and
  harvesting wild blueberries on 2\,000 acres of blueberry barrens.\nLast y
 ear alone\, Maine farmers harvested 55 million pounds of wild blueberries.
  This special fruit has more antioxidants and is much tastier than any oth
 er kind of blueberry in the world.\nTo honor our wild blueberry growers an
 d businesses and their significant contributions to the Maine economy\, I 
 declared our first-ever Wild Blueberry Weekend in 2021. Wild blueberry gro
 wers opened their doors and made fresh\, wild blueberry jams and pies and 
 other products for guests to purchase. The Wild Blueberry Commission of Ma
 ine reported that there were thousands of dollars in direct farm sales and
  thousands of visitors who enjoyed touring the places where wild blueberri
 es grow\, maybe even picking a few of their own.\nWell\, this year\, the 6
 th annual Wild Blueberry Weekend will feature farm tours and other family-
 friendly activities at 11 different farms in Cumberland\, Hancock\, Kenneb
 ec\, Knox\, Lincoln\, and Washington counties\, as well as at the Wild Blu
 eberry Heritage Center in Columbia Falls. And wild blueberry-themed menu i
 tems and products will be available throughout the weekend at restaurants\
 , ice cream shops\, breweries\, wineries\, and distilleries all across the
  state.\nTo see the full list of participants\, visit\nwww.wildblueberrywe
 ekend.com\nand\nwww.realmaine.com\nfor year-round ideas on ways you can su
 pport Maine’s agriculture community. I encourage every\nhttp://www.maine
 .gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/maines-6th-annual-wild-blueberry-we
 ekend-2026-07-24
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/maines-6th-annu
 al-wild-blueberry-weekend-2026-07-24
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:50b4b72e7aa4f587@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260717T193101Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260717
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] ICE must be Reformed or Abolished
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nWell\, on the morning of July 13th\, a Federal Enforcement Removal 
 Operations Officer with U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement\, or ICE\
 , fatally shot Johan Sebastián Guerrero in Biddeford. Mr. Guerrero was a 
 husband and a father. He was reportedly authorized to work in the United S
 tates\, and he possessed a Social Security number. And according to public
  reports\, he was apparently not the person targeted by the immigration en
 forcement operation.\nWell\, like people all across Maine\, I'm heartbroke
 n\, and I'm furious. Not simply by the violent death of a young father in 
 a quiet town\, but by the callous show of force by federal agents across t
 he country in recent months.\nWe all have questions about what happened in
  Biddeford on Monday morning\, and I have full confidence that Attorney Ge
 neral Aaron Frey and his office are conducting a thorough and impartial in
 vestigation into the facts surrounding Mr. Guerrero's death.\nInstead of m
 aking us safer\, in many cases\, federal agents are creating situations th
 at put neighborhoods in danger. Earlier this year\, Congress had a chance 
 to rein in ICE by passing reasonable oversight measures. But instead of do
 ing that\, doing what was right and necessary\, it failed to act.\nBefore 
 more families are robbed of a loved one\, this violence has to end.\nBefor
 e one community is robbed of its peace and security\, this has to end.\nBe
 fore more gunshots shatter another windshield\, steal another person's lif
 e\, and fracture the freedom of all who want peace\, this has to end.\nAnd
  before the fragile American Dream\, memorialized on hopeful parchment two
  and a half centuries ago\, is altogether abandoned\, this has to end.\nAn
 d so\, this week\, I called on Congress not simply to share our grief\, or
  express concern\, or offer prayers and condolences\, but act now to requi
 re ICE to respect the rule of law and honor our collective security across
  the country. In my letter to Maine's congressional del\nhttp://www.maine.
 gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/ice-must-be-reformed-or-abolished-20
 26-07-17
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/ice-must-be-ref
 ormed-or-abolished-2026-07-17
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b40ba67828c9410c@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260710T194926Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260710
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Investing in Accessible Housing Acro
 ss Maine
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nEvery family\, I believe\, should be able to make memories in their
  own home\, and yet\, the news is filled every day with stories about the 
 difficulties in renting or buying a house from California to Connecticut. 
 Even if you can find a place that you want\, it may well be beyond your me
 ans. Well\, here in Maine\, we're trying to tackle these problems of acces
 s and affordability\, doing everything we can at the state level\, at leas
 t\, to build up inventory and to preserve homes and apartments that are af
 fordable for families.\nIn 2023\, we released a study that says that Maine
  needs about 80\,000 new homes by 2030 to support our population increase 
 and our future economy. Using that data\, my administration and MaineHousi
 ng set some goals to keep our state on track to meet that target. And last
  month\, we received data showing that we're on track to meet the goal.\nL
 ast year\, Maine communities permitted 7\,500 new housing units—that exc
 eeds our goal by about nine percent. Annual goals will ramp up in the year
 s ahead\, but this good news demonstrates that efforts to make it easier t
 o build homes is working.\nSince I took office\, my administration has aut
 horized more than $350 million to build more homes. That's more than five 
 times what the state of Maine spent on housing production\, in total\, fro
 m 2000 to 2018. We've also worked with the legislature to enact zoning\, l
 and use\, regulatory\, and permitting reforms to allow the private sector 
 to build homes that Maine people can afford.\nWe've expanded tax relief pr
 ograms like the Property Tax Fairness Credit and the Homestead Exemption a
 nd the State Historic Rehabilitation Tax Credit. We created and extended t
 he Affordable Housing Tax Credit\, the largest state investment in housing
  in Maine's history. We created Maine's first-ever long-term funding sourc
 e for affordable housing production with revenue from real estate sales of
  over $1 million\, and that is expected to gener\nhttp://www.maine.gov/gov
 ernor/mills/news/radio_address/investing-accessible-housing-across-maine-2
 026-07-10
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/investing-acces
 sible-housing-across-maine-2026-07-10
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:09fd9e6d30786d7b@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260704T191745Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260704
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Happy Independence Day Weekend\, Ame
 rica!
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning. And happy Fourth of July.\nThis week\, we celebrate the 250th annive
 rsary of our nation's founding document. That original declaration of prin
 ciples\, that expression of passion that articulates\, among other things\
 , how we Americans are "created equal."\nOf course\, the narrow definition
  of who deserves life\, liberty\, and happiness has been expanded since th
 at time\, following the many struggles for freedom and equality that are a
 lso an important part of our American history. But 250 years ago\, that Co
 mmittee of Five crafted a simple but powerful promise\, endowing us with t
 he "unalienable rights" that belong to every American\, regardless of poli
 tics\, power\, or possessions.\nWhat do you think they envisioned? Would t
 hey ever have dreamed of a continuing nation this far in advance? And the 
 shared values that they embodied in that Declaration that still unite our 
 states and the people of this country two and a half centuries later? Woul
 d they have thought about the people who defied expectations to lead scien
 tific discovery\, and those who took a “giant leap for mankind” on the
  moon? Those who mastered flight in Kitty Hawk\, North Carolina. Those who
  marched for equal rights in Portland\, Maine\, and Montgomery\, Alabama. 
 Those who redefined athletic excellence\, and those who became the King of
  Rock and Roll and the Queen of Soul\, singing anthems that moved generati
 ons to their feet. The people who explored the frontier and rebuilt our ci
 ties after economic crises.\nFamous people and unsung heroes who keep Amer
 ica strong. Schoolteachers like my mother\, potato farmers like my grandfa
 ther\, fishermen like my great-grandfather\, and Americans all over driven
  by a belief—often tested—that our will can overcome any obstacle\, no
  matter how high. That our determination can achieve any cause\, no matter
  how distant. A “self-evident” spirit of discovery\, of bravery\, of c
 ourage\, of kindness that is at the heart of this nation\, at\nhttp://www.
 maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/happy-independence-day-weekend
 -america-2026-07-03
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/happy-independe
 nce-day-weekend-america-2026-07-03
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9af9aa025e99b45c@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260701T202235Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260626
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Fighting the Problem of Elder Abuse
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nYou know\, the abuse of vulnerable Maine people\, especially older 
 adults\, is an insidious problem. One in ten older Maine people experience
  abuse or exploitation\, most often committed by someone they actually tru
 st. But only one in 24 cases is actually reported to law enforcement.\nFar
  too often\, older adults are alone and isolated\, and they depend on only
  one or two people\, sometimes family members\, for their well-being\, and
  they're hesitant to ask for help. Eradicating abuse is a shared responsib
 ility\, and it requires state government\, law enforcement\, organizations
 \, and financial professionals to work together to protect older adults.\n
 As District Attorney and later as Attorney General\, I prosecuted many cri
 mes against older Maine people. And in 2014\, I convened a task force to c
 ombat financial exploitation of older people. With that\, we made changes 
 to judicial case management--so prioritizing these cases--changes to staff
 ing\, and changes to specialized training for law enforcement officers\, i
 n order to ferret out abuse and investigate it in a more effective and str
 eamlined fashion.\nIn 2019\, as Governor\, I signed into law "An Act to Pr
 otect Vulnerable Adults from Financial Exploitation\," which requires cert
 ain professionals who suspect financial exploitation to report those conce
 rns to the Office of Securities or Adult Protective Services. In that same
  year\, I signed an executive order to create the Elder Justice Coordinati
 ng Partnership\, and in 2022\, I created the Cabinet on Aging to make sure
  that my administration is doing everything we can to break down silos and
  take every step to protect the dignity\, safety\, and rights of older Mai
 ne people.\nRecently\, I announced the 2026 Maine Elder Justice Roadmap. T
 hat's a report that outlines the progress we've made and which recommends 
 priorities for the next administration through 2028. That progress include
 s investing $4 million in the budget with the su\nhttp://www.maine.gov/gov
 ernor/mills/news/radio_address/fighting-problem-elder-abuse-2026-06-26
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/fighting-proble
 m-elder-abuse-2026-06-26
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ad4e7a29b620e4dc@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260701T202235Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260618
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Millions in Critical Investments are
  Coming to Rural Communities in Maine
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nYou know\, rural Maine is my home. And I know firsthand that our ru
 ral communities have the people\, the work ethic\, and the ideas to build 
 a stronger future. What they haven't always had is the investment that's n
 ecessary to turn that potential into real progress.\nWell\, this week I wa
 s proud to announce that 17 rural communities and organizations across Mai
 ne have been awarded nearly $16 million in federal grants from the Norther
 n Border Regional Commission for some important economic development proje
 cts. The Northern Border Regional Commission\, along with the state of Mai
 ne\, provides targeted federal investment for economic and community devel
 opment projects in counties that are in states along the northern border.\
 nSince the Commission was formed through legislation sponsored by then-Con
 gressman Michael Michaud in 2008\, Maine has received more than $100 milli
 on for hundreds of projects in rural communities. The projects that will r
 eceive funding through these latest awards will enhance economic developme
 nt and build workforce housing\, expand childcare\, and fund infrastructur
 e improvements. For example:\nBrooksville will use its grant to build the 
 town's first all-tide public pier landing and boat ramp at Betsy's Cove.\n
 Tree Street Youth in Lewiston will build a new neighborhood center with ch
 ildcare\, a community meeting space\, and youth workforce training.\nFarmi
 ngton will benefit from a grant to build a multi-use pedestrian and snowmo
 bile bridge over the Sandy River\, extending the Whistle Stop Trail to dow
 ntown.\nAnd the Northern Maine Development Commission will create "Aroosto
 ok Rising\," a two-year project to recruit talented workers.\nI recommende
 d these projects for federal funding after a competitive review conducted 
 by my Department of Economic and Community Development. And recently\, the
  Commission--the Northern Border Regional Commission--also announced award
 s in Maine through its Forest Economy \nhttp://www.maine.gov/governor/mill
 s/news/radio_address/millions-critical-investments-are-coming-rural-commun
 ities-maine-2026-06-18
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/millions-critic
 al-investments-are-coming-rural-communities-maine-2026-06-18
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:76891f8fb94adb31@gov-press-releases.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260701T202235Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260615
SUMMARY:[Governor / Executive Branch] Protecting Our Homes and Infrastruct
 ure from Extreme Weather
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nWhen I took office nearly eight years ago\, I promised that Maine w
 on't wait to take action to protect our state\, our people\, and our econo
 my from the dangers of extreme weather events caused by pollution and chan
 ges in our environment. And I promised that we would fight to preserve thi
 s special place that we call home for the benefit of our children and gran
 dchildren.\nWe know that the stakes couldn't be higher. Look no further th
 an the devastating storms of just a few years ago that impacted our commun
 ities\, our people\, and our economy. I've made climate action a cornersto
 ne of my administration. We created the Maine Climate Council. We issued a
  groundbreaking climate action plan\, and we dedicated millions of federal
  and state dollars to make that plan a reality.\nIn 2025\, we made the sin
 gle largest investment in storm recovery by any administration in Maine hi
 story\, and we created the State Resilience Office to build better\, stron
 ger public infrastructure in order to withstand future storms. That office
  manages the Community Resilience Partnership\, which includes more than 3
 00 communities that we’re helping. And it has awarded nearly $25 million
  in state and federal funds for local projects to prepare for flooding\, f
 or storm surge\, for rising sea levels\, and other climate events t\nhttp:
 //www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/protecting-our-homes-and
 -infrastructure-extreme-weather-2026-06-15
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/protecting-our-
 homes-and-infrastructure-extreme-weather-2026-06-15
CATEGORIES:Governor / Executive Branch
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
